ntdll: a dump that drowns the channel, and a fault that names nothing.

Two changes to the `virtual` channel, both about being able to read it.

FIRST, the view dump gets its own channel. `VIRTUAL_DEBUG_DUMP_VIEW` fires on
every view create, split and protection change, and under FEX the JIT changes
protections continuously, so `+virtual` on a real title is not a channel anyone
can read. Measured on a Requiem session: 75,505 of the first 89,000 lines were
this macro -- 85% of the log. It filled a 32 MB head budget in 90 seconds and
dropped 1.87 million lines, taking the records `+virtual` exists to carry
(NtAllocateVirtualMemory and its neighbours, 6% of the same sample) into the
elision with it.

Splitting rather than deleting, because the dump is the right instrument for a
layout question and the wrong one for every other question on the channel;
`+virtual_views` still asks for it by name. dump_view's own TRACEs move with it,
since gating the call while leaving them on the default channel would make
`+virtual_views` alone print nothing. Nothing below it is silenced: second place
in that census is 4.5%, and quieting more would hide the record rather than
reveal it.

SECOND, a fault that could not be repaired says where it landed.
`virtual_handle_fault` either repairs a fault or decides it cannot, and the
decision was invisible -- the thread carried STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION out to the
guest, which reports an address and nothing about the memory at it. Reading that
back afterwards cost a full `+virtual` session, which the change above exists
because of.

It is silent until it fires, and it fires on a fault that is already fatal,
which is also why the walk over the view's pages is not bounded.

This is what named the cause of #51 one run later, in the branch for an address
no view covers at all:

    virtual_handle_fault unrepaired fault at 0x6fffe3003c, no view covers it

See patches/wine/0044.

THE FIRST VERSION OF THAT REPORT WAS ITSELF A FLOOD, which is worth recording
because this patch's other half exists to fix exactly that mistake. It reported
every unrepaired fault, at ERR, over three lines. But "unrepaired by this layer"
is not "fatal" -- FEX handles most of them itself, one session taking 522 on
re9.exe's own image from write-watch alone -- so a session ended with

    EV … and 30042 further distinct errors, not listed …

the error digest useless, and Vessel's session-stopped banner quoting `view
protect --rw-`, the third line of a three-line report, which says nothing on its
own. The real exit-code-1 cause was buried underneath it.

So the report is now split by what the fault means, and is one line either way:
an address no view covers is *always* wrong -- a pointer into nothing, which no
handler can repair -- and stays an ERR; a fault inside a view is ordinary
emulator business and drops to WARN on `virtual`, off unless asked for, with the
page walk inside the WARN_ON test so an unasked-for channel costs nothing.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -105,6 +105,7 @@
 WINE_DECLARE_DEBUG_CHANNEL(module);
 WINE_DECLARE_DEBUG_CHANNEL(virtual_ranges);
 WINE_DECLARE_DEBUG_CHANNEL(virtstat);
+WINE_DECLARE_DEBUG_CHANNEL(virtual_views);
 
 /* Gdb integration, in loader/main.c */
 static struct r_debug *wine_r_debug;
@@ -342,7 +343,23 @@
 #define ROUND_ADDR(addr,mask) ((void *)((UINT_PTR)(addr) & ~(UINT_PTR)(mask)))
 #define ROUND_SIZE(addr,size,mask) (((SIZE_T)(size) + ((UINT_PTR)(addr) & (mask)) + (mask)) & ~(UINT_PTR)(mask))
 
-#define VIRTUAL_DEBUG_DUMP_VIEW(view) do { if (TRACE_ON(virtual)) dump_view(view); } while (0)
+/* Vessel: the view dump is its own channel, because it drowned the one it
+ * shared.
+ *
+ * This fires on every view create, split and protection change, and under FEX
+ * the JIT changes protections constantly -- so `+virtual` on a real title is
+ * not a channel anyone can read. Measured on a Requiem session: 75,505 of the
+ * first 89,000 lines were this macro, 85% of the log. It filled a 32 MB head
+ * budget in 90 seconds and dropped 1.87 million lines, taking the records the
+ * channel exists for -- NtAllocateVirtualMemory and its neighbours, 6% of the
+ * same sample -- into the elision with it. After the split the same 8 MB held
+ * 9,208 allocation records instead of 1,239.
+ *
+ * Splitting rather than deleting: the dump is the right instrument for a
+ * layout question and the wrong one for every other question on the channel.
+ * `+virtual_views` still asks for it by name.
+ */
+#define VIRTUAL_DEBUG_DUMP_VIEW(view) do { if (TRACE_ON(virtual_views)) dump_view(view); } while (0)
 #define VIRTUAL_DEBUG_DUMP_RANGES() do { if (TRACE_ON(virtual_ranges)) dump_free_ranges(); } while (0)
 
 #ifndef MAP_NORESERVE
@@ -1539,34 +1556,34 @@
     char *addr = view->base;
     BYTE prot = get_page_vprot( addr );
 
-    TRACE( "View: %p - %p %s", addr, addr + view->size - 1, get_prot_str(view->protect) );
+    TRACE_(virtual_views)( "View: %p - %p %s", addr, addr + view->size - 1, get_prot_str(view->protect) );
     if (view->protect & VPROT_NATIVE)
-        TRACE(" (native)\n");
+        TRACE_(virtual_views)(" (native)\n");
     else if (view->protect & VPROT_SYSTEM)
-        TRACE( " (builtin image)\n" );
+        TRACE_(virtual_views)( " (builtin image)\n" );
     else if (view->protect & VPROT_FREE_PLACEHOLDER)
-        TRACE( " (placeholder)\n" );
+        TRACE_(virtual_views)( " (placeholder)\n" );
     else if (view->protect & SEC_IMAGE)
-        TRACE( " (image)\n" );
+        TRACE_(virtual_views)( " (image)\n" );
     else if (view->protect & SEC_FILE)
-        TRACE( " (file)\n" );
+        TRACE_(virtual_views)( " (file)\n" );
     else if (view->protect & (SEC_RESERVE | SEC_COMMIT))
-        TRACE( " (anonymous)\n" );
+        TRACE_(virtual_views)( " (anonymous)\n" );
     else
-        TRACE( " (valloc)\n");
+        TRACE_(virtual_views)( " (valloc)\n");
 
     for (count = i = 1; i < view->size >> page_shift; i++, count++)
     {
         BYTE next = get_page_vprot( addr + (count << page_shift) );
         if (next == prot) continue;
-        TRACE( "      %p - %p %s\n",
+        TRACE_(virtual_views)( "      %p - %p %s\n",
                  addr, addr + (count << page_shift) - 1, get_prot_str(prot) );
         addr += (count << page_shift);
         prot = next;
         count = 0;
     }
     if (count)
-        TRACE( "      %p - %p %s\n",
+        TRACE_(virtual_views)( "      %p - %p %s\n",
                  addr, addr + (count << page_shift) - 1, get_prot_str(prot) );
 }
 
@@ -4957,6 +4974,63 @@
                 ret = STATUS_SUCCESS;
         }
     }
+    /* Vessel: a fault we did not repair is worth one line about where it landed.
+     *
+     * Everything above either repairs the fault or decides it cannot, and the
+     * decision was invisible: the thread carried STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION out to
+     * the guest, which reports an address and nothing about the memory at it.
+     *
+     * THE SPLIT BETWEEN ERR AND WARN IS THE WHOLE DESIGN, and the first version
+     * of this got it wrong in a way worth recording. It reported every unrepaired
+     * fault, at ERR, over three lines. But "unrepaired by this layer" is not
+     * "fatal" -- FEX handles most of these itself, one Requiem session taking 522
+     * on the game's own image from write-watch alone -- so a single session ended
+     * with `30042 further distinct errors, not listed`, the digest useless, and
+     * the session-stopped banner quoting `view protect --rw-`, which was line
+     * three of three and says nothing on its own. An instrument that buries the
+     * failure it was built to find is the exact defect the channel split above
+     * exists to fix.
+     *
+     * So: an address that no view covers is *always* wrong -- a pointer into
+     * nothing, which no handler can repair and which nothing legitimate produces
+     * -- and that is the ERR. A fault inside a view is ordinary emulator business
+     * and drops to WARN on `virtual`, off unless asked for. One line either way,
+     * because a multi-line report gets quoted by its last line.
+     *
+     * This is what named the cause of the Requiem crash, in the ERR branch:
+     *
+     *   unrepaired read fault at 0x6fffe3003c, no view covers it
+     *
+     * -- ntdll's stale base plus 0x3c, the DOS header of a module that had been
+     * relocated out from under the pointer. See patches/wine/0044.
+     *
+     * The page walk sits inside the WARN_ON test so that a channel nobody asked
+     * for costs nothing, and get_prot_str is copied once because it returns a
+     * static buffer: two calls in one printf would both print the second answer.
+     */
+    if (ret == STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION)
+    {
+        struct file_view *view = find_view( addr, 0 );
+        const char *how = (err == EXCEPTION_WRITE_FAULT) ? "write" :
+                          (err == EXCEPTION_EXECUTE_FAULT) ? "exec" : "read";
+
+        if (!view) ERR( "unrepaired %s fault at %p, no view covers it\n", how, addr );
+        else if (WARN_ON(virtual))
+        {
+            char *view_base = view->base;
+            SIZE_T i, pages = view->size >> page_shift, committed = 0;
+            char page_prot[8];
+
+            for (i = 0; i < pages; i++)
+                if (get_page_vprot( view_base + (i << page_shift) ) & VPROT_COMMITTED)
+                    committed += (SIZE_T)1 << page_shift;
+
+            strcpy( page_prot, get_prot_str( get_host_page_vprot( page )));
+            WARN( "unrepaired %s fault at %p in view %p size %08lx, %08lx committed, page %s, view %s\n",
+                  how, addr, view_base, view->size, committed, page_prot, get_prot_str( view->protect ));
+        }
+    }
+
     mutex_unlock( &virtual_mutex );
     rec->ExceptionCode = ret;
     return ret;
